@node Markets
@chapter Markets

The market screens draw price charts.  Every instrument is drawn once
per range, side by side: by default over the last month (4- or
6-hourly points) and over the last half year (daily points).  Each
instrument names the screen it belongs to, and each screen gets its own
tab; by default @samp{XAUT · TON} shows XAUT/USDT (Tether Gold) and
TON/USDT, and @samp{BTC · RUB} shows BTC/USD and USD/RUB, one
instrument per row.  When the terminal is too narrow for the ranges to
sit side by side, the charts are stacked in one column.

Each chart's first line shows the last price and the change over the
range in absolute terms and in percent (green when up, red when down),
with the range's high and low on the right.  The second line adds the
first price of the range (@samp{open}), the average, how far the last
price is from the high and from the low, and the daily volatility: the
standard deviation of the returns between consecutive points, scaled
to one day.  Items that do not fit are left out.  Below them is a
Braille line plot with a labelled price axis; the time axis is labelled
with days for a week, Mondays for a month and month names for half a
year.

The frame title gives the range, how long ago the chart was fetched and
the provider that served it, for example @samp{BTC/USD · 1 month · 3h
ago · coinbase BTC-USD}.  The age comes before the provider so that it
survives when a narrow frame cuts the title short.  The title turns
yellow when the newest point is older than the chart's
@code{max_age_hours} plus one refresh interval, which means the data
has not been updated as often as it should be.

@section Refreshing and the cache

Prices are fetched twice a day by default (@code{interval = 43200}).
Every successful response is saved in the cache directory (@pxref{Sources and caching}), one file per chart and range, together with the provider that served
it.  When raspdash starts it redraws the charts from these files at
once, and fetches again only when they are older than the interval, so a
restart or a reboot costs no requests.  The cache of a chart is ignored
when its provider is no longer listed for that chart, and the whole
cache is ignored when any chart has none, so a newly added chart is
fetched without waiting.

Press @kbd{r} on the screen to fetch at once.  When some charts fail,
the others are updated anyway, the failed ones keep their previous data
with @samp{refresh failed} in their first line, and the footer names
them; raspdash then retries sooner than the interval, backing off after
repeated failures.

@section Providers and fallback

Each chart has an ordered list of providers.  raspdash asks them in
turn and uses the first answer that has at least two points and whose
newest point is no older than the chart's @code{max_age_hours} plus
the spacing of the range's points (6 hours for a month, a day for half a
year, since the newest daily candle starts at midnight).  An
error, an empty answer or a stale one moves on to the next provider; if
all of them fail, the chart shows every provider's error.

@table @code
@item binance
Binance spot klines: hourly for a week, 4-hourly for a month (6-hourly
from Coinbase), daily for half a year.  @var{symbol} like @samp{BTCUSDT}.
Binance stopped updating TONUSDT at the end of June 2026 while still
listing it, which is why TON/USDT tries it last and why the age check
exists.
@item bybit
Bybit v5 spot klines: hourly for a week, 4-hourly for a month (6-hourly
from Coinbase), daily for half a year.  @var{symbol} like @samp{BTCUSDT}.
@item okx
OKX candles: hourly for a week, 4-hourly for a month (6-hourly
from Coinbase), daily for half a year.  @var{symbol} like @samp{BTC-USDT}.
@item coinbase
Coinbase Exchange candles: hourly for a week, 4-hourly for a month (6-hourly
from Coinbase), daily for half a year.  @var{symbol} like @samp{BTC-USD}.
This is a real US dollar market, unlike the USDT pairs.
@item kraken
Kraken OHLC: hourly for a week, 4-hourly for a month (6-hourly
from Coinbase), daily for half a year.  @var{symbol} like @samp{TONUSD}, @samp{XBTUSD},
@samp{PAXGUSD}.
@item coingecko
CoinGecko market chart: hourly up to 90 days, daily beyond.  @var{symbol} is the coin's
CoinGecko id (@samp{the-open-network}, @samp{bitcoin}, @samp{tether-gold});
the optional key @code{vs} picks the quote currency (default
@samp{usd}).
@item cbr
The Bank of Russia's official daily rate.  @var{symbol} is the CBR
currency code, @samp{R01235} for the US dollar.
@end table

None of these services needs an account or a key.

@section Caveats

@itemize @bullet
@item
Yahoo Finance is not supported.  It has no public API; the endpoint its
web pages use answered @samp{429 Too Many Requests} for hours at a time,
so it was removed.
@item
XAUT (Tether Gold) is a token backed by one troy ounce of gold held by
Tether.  It follows the spot gold price closely but is not the same
thing: its price includes the market's view of the issuer, and it
trades around the clock, so the chart has no weekend gaps.
@item
The CBR rate is set once per working day, so the USD/RUB chart, which uses only this rate, has
about five points a week; the rate dated a given day applies from
Moscow midnight of that day.  Since June 2024 the Moscow Exchange has no
USD trading, so there is no official intraday USD/RUB source.
@item
TON/USDT is drawn from TON/USD markets (Kraken, CoinGecko) because
Kraken lists no TON/USDT pair and Binance's has stopped; USDT trades
within a fraction of a percent of the dollar.
@end itemize

@section Configuration

@example
[markets]
interval = 43200
timezone = "Europe/Moscow"
ranges = ["month", "half-year"]

[[markets.charts]]
name = "TON/USDT"
screen = "XAUT · TON"
icon = "\U000F01C8"
decimals = 3
max_age_hours = 6
providers = [
  @{ type = "kraken", symbol = "TONUSD" @},
  @{ type = "coingecko", symbol = "the-open-network" @},
  @{ type = "binance", symbol = "TONUSDT" @},
]
@end example

@table @code
@item interval
Seconds between fetches.  Default 43200 (12 hours).
@item timezone
Time zone whose midnights label the time axis.  Default
@samp{Europe/Moscow}.
@item ranges
The ranges every instrument is drawn over, in column order:
@samp{week}, @samp{month} and @samp{half-year}.  Default
@samp{["month", "half-year"]}.
@item charts
An array of tables, one per instrument, in screen order.  When it is
absent the four default instruments are used; when it is present, only
the instruments it lists are shown.
@end table

Each chart takes:

@table @code
@item name
The title, such as @samp{BTC/USD}.  Required.  It also names the cache
file, so renaming a chart refetches it.
@item screen
The title of the screen the instrument is drawn on.  Instruments with
the same @code{screen} share it, one row each, and screens appear in
the order their first instrument is listed, in place of
@samp{markets} in @code{[app] screens}.  Default @samp{Markets}.
@item color
The colour of the plot line: @samp{yellow}, @samp{green}, @samp{blue},
@samp{cyan}, @samp{magenta}, @samp{red}, @samp{orange} or
@samp{white}.  The defaults draw XAUT in yellow, TON in blue and the
dollar pairs (BTC/USD, USD/RUB) in green.  Without it, instruments take
the four series colours in turn.
@item icon
A Nerd Font glyph shown before the name when @code{[app] icons} is
@samp{nerd}, written as a TOML escape such as @samp{"\U000F0813"}.
Optional.
@item decimals
Decimals of the prices shown.  When absent they follow the price's
magnitude.
@item max_age_hours
The newest point of an acceptable answer must be at most this old.
Default 6.  Use about 80 for instruments that do not trade at weekends
and about 100 for the daily CBR rate.
@item providers
The providers to try, in order, as inline tables with @code{type},
@code{symbol} and, for @code{coingecko}, @code{vs}.  Required.  An
unknown type is an error when raspdash starts.
@end table

To add an instrument, add a @code{[[markets.charts]]} table (copy the
four defaults first, since a @code{charts} array replaces them).  For
Ethereum, for example:

@example
[[markets.charts]]
name = "ETH/USD"
screen = "ETH"
decimals = 0
providers = [
  @{ type = "coinbase", symbol = "ETH-USD" @},
  @{ type = "kraken", symbol = "ETHUSD" @},
  @{ type = "coingecko", symbol = "ethereum" @},
]
@end example

An instrument on a screen of its own gets the whole screen for its
ranges.

@section Internals

@file{src/markets.cpp} has one parser per provider
(@code{parse_binance}, @code{parse_kraken}, @dots{}), each turning a
response body into points (candle start time, close) sorted by time
with duplicates removed.  Exchanges that list newest first are sorted
like the rest.  Prices written as JSON strings, as most exchanges do,
are read with @code{std::from_chars}.  Error responses (an object where
Binance or Coinbase normally send an array, a non-zero @code{retCode}
from Bybit, @code{code} other than @samp{"0"} from OKX, a non-empty
@code{error} array from Kraken, a body
without @code{<ValCurs} from CBR) become exceptions with the provider's
message.  Kraken keys its result by its canonical pair name, which may
differ from the one requested (@samp{XBTUSD} comes back as
@samp{XXBTZUSD}), so the parser takes the first key that is not
@samp{last}.  The CBR document is windows-1251, but every byte read from it
is ASCII; its values use a decimal comma and are divided by
@code{Nominal}.

@code{expand_ranges} turns the configured instruments into charts, one
per instrument and range, grouped by screen; @code{install_markets}
then adds one source for all of them and one screen per screen title.
A @code{Range} has an id, a label, a length (7, 30 or 182 days) and the
spacing of its points.

@code{provider_url} builds each request for the chart's range before
the current time, choosing the candle size and count per provider.  The
CBR request reaches three days further back and one day ahead, because
weekends have no record and the latest rate is dated tomorrow.

@code{fetch_chart} walks a chart's providers, applies @code{last_span}
and the age check, and records each failure, without the URL that
transport errors start with, so the final error stays readable.
@code{last_span} keeps the points within the range's length of the
newest one: the window follows the data rather than the clock, so a
chart restored from a twelve-hour-old cache still shows the whole
range.  @code{statistics} computes the numbers of the chart's first two
lines.

The source fetches all charts concurrently, one thread per chart, and
stores per chart either a new result or the error next to the previous
result.  It throws after storing when any chart failed, so the footer
shows the failure and the poller retries sooner.  The cache file of a
chart (@file{chart-@var{slug}-@var{range}.json}, where the slug is the
lower-cased name with other characters turned into dashes) holds a JSON object with
the provider's @code{type}, @code{symbol}, @code{vs} and the raw
@code{body}; restoring parses the body again with the same parser.

Charts are plotted against time, not point index, so the day labels line
up across charts and the weekend gaps in forex data stay visible.  A
segment is not drawn across a gap longer than four times the median
spacing of the points: the weekend in hourly futures data is left empty
instead of being bridged by a line through prices that never traded,
while the daily CBR series, whose weekend gap is three times its usual
spacing, stays connected.  The price axis is padded by 4% on each side
and labelled with ``nice'' steps (1, 2, 2.5 or 5 times a power of ten)
that fall inside the padded range; rows without a dot get a dotted guide
line at each label.  Day labels go at local midnights and are skipped
where they would overlap or run past the frame.
